window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([
{t:"Communication",q:"Every method of communication, human or machine, shares three common elements. Which set names them correctly?",o:["Source, destination and channel","Sender, protocol and acknowledgement","Encoder, decoder and cable","Client, server and switch"],a:0,e:"Source (sender), destination (receiver) and channel (media) are the three elements. Everything else in networking is a rule layered on top of those three. Protocols and acknowledgements are rules governing the communication, not elements of it; client/server is one application model, not a universal element."},
{t:"Communication",q:"Encoding is best described as which of the following?",o:["Converting information into another acceptable form for transmission","Encrypting data so that only the recipient can read it","Compressing data to reduce the size of a message","Numbering segments so they can be reassembled in order"],a:0,e:"Encoding converts information into an acceptable form for transmission, and decoding reverses it to interpret the information. Encryption and compression are presentation layer functions, and numbering segments is sequencing, performed by TCP."},
{t:"Communication",q:"Which message timing rule determines WHEN a device is allowed to send a message?",o:["Access method","Flow control","Response timeout","Message encapsulation"],a:0,e:"Access method determines when someone can send. Flow control manages the rate of transmission and how much may be sent; response timeout manages how long a device waits when it hears no reply. Collisions arise from the access method: proactive protocols try to prevent them, reactive protocols recover after one."},
{t:"Communication",q:"Which delivery option is used in IPv4 networks but is NOT available in IPv6?",o:["Broadcast","Multicast","Unicast","Anycast"],a:0,e:"IPv6 has no broadcast. Unicast (one-to-one) and multicast (one-to-many) exist in both; IPv6 additionally adds anycast, a unicast address assigned to multiple devices. IPv6 replaces IPv4 broadcast functions with multicast, such as the all-nodes address ff02::1."},
{t:"Communication",q:"A protocol category that enables routers to exchange route information, compare paths and select the best path is classified as which type?",o:["Routing","Network communications","Service discovery","Network security"],a:0,e:"The four protocol types are network communications (enabling devices to communicate), network security (authentication, integrity, encryption), routing (exchanging route information and choosing paths) and service discovery (automatic detection of devices or services)."},
{t:"Communication",q:"Which protocol function uniquely labels each transmitted segment of data?",o:["Sequencing","Addressing","Error detection","Flow control"],a:0,e:"Sequencing uniquely labels each segment so the message can be reassembled in the right order at the destination. Addressing identifies sender and receiver; error detection determines whether data was corrupted; flow control keeps data moving at an efficient rate."},
{t:"Standards",q:"Which organisation develops, updates and maintains internet and TCP/IP technologies?",o:["IETF","ICANN","IEEE","ITU-T"],a:0,e:"The IETF maintains the TCP/IP suite and developed IPv6. ICANN coordinates IP address allocation and domain name management; IANA does that work for ICANN and assigns port numbers. The IEEE creates networking standards such as 802.3 Ethernet, and ITU-T defines video compression, IPTV and DSL standards."},
{t:"Standards",q:"Open standards are promoted because they encourage which three outcomes?",o:["Interoperability, competition and innovation","Security, encryption and privacy","Speed, bandwidth and throughput","Redundancy, resilience and uptime"],a:0,e:"Standards organisations are vendor-neutral non-profit bodies established to develop and promote open standards, which encourage interoperability, competition and innovation. TCP/IP is both an open standard (freely available to any vendor) and standards-based (endorsed by the industry to ensure interoperability)."},
{t:"OSI model",q:"Which OSI layer describes the mechanical, electrical, functional and procedural means to transmit bits across physical connections?",o:["Physical","Data Link","Network","Transport"],a:0,e:"Layer 1, Physical. Data Link (layer 2) exchanges frames over a common media; Network (layer 3) exchanges pieces of data between identified end devices; Transport (layer 4) segments, transfers and reassembles data for individual communications."},
{t:"OSI model",q:"Which OSI layer provides for a common representation of data, including compression and encryption?",o:["Presentation","Session","Application","Transport"],a:0,e:"The Presentation layer (6) has three functions: formatting data into a compatible form, compressing data so the destination can decompress it, and encrypting for transmission with decryption on receipt. The Session layer (5) creates and maintains dialogs and restarts disrupted sessions."},
{t:"OSI model",q:"The TCP/IP Network Access layer corresponds to which OSI layers?",o:["Data Link and Physical","Network and Data Link","Physical only","Application, Presentation and Session"],a:0,e:"OSI splits TCP/IP's Network Access layer into Data Link and Physical, and splits TCP/IP's Application layer into Application, Presentation and Session. The TCP/IP suite does not specify which protocols to use over a physical medium, which is exactly why OSI layers 1 and 2 are needed to describe media access."},
{t:"OSI model",q:"Which TCP/IP model layer determines the best path through the network?",o:["Internet","Transport","Network Access","Application"],a:0,e:"The Internet layer determines the best path and maps to OSI layer 3. Transport supports communication between devices across diverse networks; Network Access controls the hardware and media; Application represents data to the user plus encoding and dialog control."},
{t:"Encapsulation",q:"What is the correct order of PDU names as data passes DOWN the protocol stack?",o:["Data, segment, packet, frame, bits","Bits, frame, packet, segment, data","Data, packet, segment, frame, bits","Frame, packet, segment, data, bits"],a:0,e:"Data, segment, packet, frame, bits. Each layer adds its header as data moves down, and the PDU takes a new name to reflect its new function. De-encapsulation at the receiver removes those headers in the reverse order: Ethernet, then IP, then transport, then the application data."},
{t:"Encapsulation",q:"Segmenting messages provides two primary benefits. Which pair is correct?",o:["Increases speed and increases efficiency","Increases security and reduces cost","Reduces latency and removes the need for addressing","Guarantees delivery and prevents collisions"],a:0,e:"Speed increases because large amounts of data can cross the network without tying up a communications link; efficiency increases because only segments that fail to arrive need retransmitting, not the entire data stream. Multiplexing then interleaves multiple streams of segmented data together."},
{t:"Encapsulation",q:"Which protocol is responsible for sequencing the individual segments so a message can be reassembled?",o:["TCP","IP","Ethernet","HTTP"],a:0,e:"TCP sequences segments using sequence numbers in each header. IP delivers packets globally but is connectionless and cannot realign out-of-sequence packets; Ethernet delivers frames between NICs on the same LAN; HTTP governs how a web server and web client interact."},
{t:"Addressing",q:"As an IP packet travels from source to destination across several routers, what happens to the source and destination IP addresses?",o:["They stay the same the whole way (unless NAT is used)","They are rewritten by every router","Only the source address changes at each hop","Both are replaced with MAC addresses at each hop"],a:0,e:"IP addressing does not change from source to destination — NAT is the one exception, which is precisely why NAT is described as breaking end-to-end connectivity. It is the data link (MAC) addressing that is stripped and rewritten in a new frame at every router hop."},
{t:"Addressing",q:"A host sends a packet to a device on a remote network. What will the destination MAC address in the frame be?",o:["The MAC address of the default gateway","The MAC address of the remote destination device","FF-FF-FF-FF-FF-FF","The MAC address of the source device"],a:0,e:"A data link frame cannot be sent directly to a remote host, so it is addressed to the default gateway — the nearest router interface. The router then removes the received Layer 2 information and adds new data link information before forwarding out the exit interface. The destination IP still names the far-away host."},
{t:"Physical layer",q:"What does the physical layer accept from the data link layer?",o:["A complete frame, which it encodes as signals on the media","A segment, which it splits into packets","Raw application data, which it formats","A routing table, which it uses to choose an interface"],a:0,e:"The physical layer accepts a complete frame from the data link layer and encodes it as a series of signals transmitted onto the local media. Those encoded bits are then received by either an end device or an intermediate device."},
{t:"Physical layer",q:"Bandwidth is most accurately defined as which of the following?",o:["The capacity of a medium to carry data in a given time","The speed at which bits travel through the medium","The measured rate of successful data transfer","Throughput minus protocol overhead"],a:0,e:"Bandwidth is capacity, not speed. In both 10 Mb/s and 100 Mb/s Ethernet the bits travel at the speed of electricity; what differs is the number of bits transmitted per second. The measured transfer rate is throughput, and throughput minus session, acknowledgement and encapsulation overhead is goodput."},
{t:"Physical layer",q:"Throughput on a link is usually lower than the stated bandwidth. Which factor does NOT contribute to that difference?",o:["The colour of the cable jacket","The amount of traffic on the link","The type of traffic being carried","Latency created by network devices along the path"],a:0,e:"Throughput rarely matches the specified bandwidth because of the amount of traffic, the type of traffic and the latency added by devices between source and destination. Jacket colour is a convention for identifying cable types — yellow for single-mode fibre, orange or aqua for multimode — and has no effect on performance."},
{t:"Media",q:"Which copper impairment is countered by twisting opposing circuit wire pairs together?",o:["Crosstalk","Attenuation","EMI from fluorescent lights","Signal reflection at the connector"],a:0,e:"Crosstalk is the disturbance caused by the electric or magnetic field of a signal on one wire affecting an adjacent wire, and twisting the pairs cancels it. Attenuation is countered by strict distance limitations, and EMI or RFI is countered by shielding the cable."},
{t:"Media",q:"UTP cable has no shielding. What mechanism protects it from EMI and RFI instead?",o:["Cancellation, because paired wires have opposite magnetic fields","A grounded drain wire running through the cable","The plastic jacket, which blocks electromagnetic waves","Error correction performed by the NIC"],a:0,e:"When two wires in a circuit are placed close together their magnetic fields are exact opposites and cancel out outside EMI and RFI. The number of twists per wire pair is deliberately varied to enhance this cancellation effect. STP adds actual foil shielding, but costs more and is harder to install."},
{t:"Media",q:"Which UTP category adds a separator between each pair of wires to allow higher speeds?",o:["Cat 6","Cat 3","Cat 5","Cat 5e"],a:0,e:"Cat 6 includes an added separator between each pair, supporting 1000 Mb/s up to 10 Gb/s, though 10 Gb/s is not recommended. Cat 3 is used for voice and phone lines, Cat 5 supports 100 Mb/s, and Cat 5e supports 1000 Mb/s. All of these follow the TIA/EIA-568 cabling standard."},
{t:"Media",q:"Which property is unique to fibre-optic cabling among the media studied?",o:["It is completely immune to EMI and RFI","It can carry data in both directions on a single strand","It requires no connectors","It suffers no attenuation over any distance"],a:0,e:"Fibre encodes bits as light pulses and is completely immune to EMI and RFI. It is not bidirectional on one strand — light travels in only one direction, so two fibres are required for full-duplex. It does experience attenuation, just far less than copper, and it uses ST, SC and LC connectors."},
{t:"Media",q:"In a fibre-optic cable, which layer acts like a mirror to keep light within the core?",o:["Cladding","Buffer","Jacket","Strengthening material"],a:0,e:"The cladding reflects light back into the core, keeping it there as it travels down the fibre. The buffer shields core and cladding from damage, the strengthening material stops the cable stretching when pulled, and the jacket protects against abrasion, moisture and contaminants."}
]);
window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([
{t:"Wireless",q:"Which wireless standard uses CSMA/CA, requiring the NIC to wait until the channel is clear?",o:["IEEE 802.11 (Wi-Fi)","IEEE 802.15 (Bluetooth)","IEEE 802.16 (WiMAX)","IEEE 802.3 (Ethernet)"],a:0,e:"Wi-Fi is 802.11 and uses Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance. 802.15 is Bluetooth, a WPAN using device pairing over 1 to 100 metres; 802.16 is WiMAX for wireless broadband access; 802.3 is wired Ethernet, which uses CSMA/CD in half-duplex."},
{t:"Wireless",q:"Which concern arises because only one wireless device can send or receive at a time?",o:["Shared medium","Coverage area","Interference","Security"],a:0,e:"Wireless is a shared medium among all wireless users, which is why WLANs operate in half-duplex. Coverage area is limited by building materials and terrain; interference comes from devices such as fluorescent lights and microwave ovens; security is the risk of unauthorised devices accessing the transmission."},
{t:"Data link",q:"Which data link sublayer identifies which network layer protocol is being used for the frame?",o:["LLC — Logical Link Control","MAC — Media Access Control","PHY — Physical sublayer","SNAP — Subnetwork Access Protocol"],a:0,e:"LLC communicates with the network layer and identifies the Layer 3 protocol, which is what allows IPv4 and IPv6 to share the same interface and media. The MAC sublayer defines the media access processes performed by the hardware and provides data link addressing."},
{t:"Data link",q:"The MAC sublayer's data encapsulation role provides three functions. Which is one of them?",o:["Frame delimiting","Path determination","Session establishment","Name resolution"],a:0,e:"Data encapsulation provides frame delimiting, addressing and error detection. The MAC sublayer's other responsibility is media access control — controlling frame placement on and off the media, and media recovery. Path determination is Layer 3 and session establishment is Layer 5."},
{t:"Data link",q:"Which statement about duplex operation on modern switches is correct?",o:["Ethernet switches operate full-duplex by default but drop to half-duplex when connected to a hub","Ethernet switches always operate half-duplex","Switches cannot negotiate duplex and must be configured manually","Full-duplex requires CSMA/CD to be enabled"],a:0,e:"Switches default to full-duplex but can operate half-duplex when connecting to a device such as an Ethernet hub. Autonegotiation lets two devices exchange speed and duplex capabilities and choose the highest performance mode. Full-duplex removes the need for CSMA/CD entirely rather than requiring it."},
{t:"Data link",q:"A duplex mismatch occurs when which condition exists?",o:["One port runs half-duplex while the other runs full-duplex","Both ports run at different speeds but the same duplex","Both ports run full-duplex at 1 Gb/s","A crossover cable is used instead of a straight-through cable"],a:0,e:"A duplex mismatch is one port at half-duplex and the other at full-duplex, and it is a common cause of Ethernet performance problems. Autonegotiation usually prevents it. Cable type mismatches are handled by auto-MDIX, which detects the cable and configures the interface accordingly."},
{t:"Data link",q:"CSMA/CD and CSMA/CA differ mainly in their timing relative to a collision. Which is correct?",o:["CSMA/CD acts after a collision occurs; CSMA/CA acts before","CSMA/CD acts before a collision; CSMA/CA acts after","Both act only after a collision","Neither is related to collisions"],a:0,e:"CSMA/CD detects collisions and comes into effect after one occurs, corrupting both transmissions and forcing a resend. CSMA/CA does not detect collisions at all — it checks whether the media is clear and waits before transmitting, so it acts before a collision could happen."},
{t:"Data link",q:"Which media access method gives each node its own time to use the medium, as in legacy Token Ring LANs?",o:["Controlled access","Contention-based access","Full-duplex access","Multiplexed access"],a:0,e:"Controlled access gives each node its own turn, and legacy Token Ring is the classic example. Contention-based access is CSMA/CD and CSMA/CA, where devices compete for the medium. Switched Ethernet uses neither, because full-duplex operation removes contention."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"Which IEEE standards define Ethernet?",o:["802.2 and 802.3","802.11 and 802.15","802.1 and 802.16","802.3 and 802.11"],a:0,e:"Ethernet is defined in IEEE 802.2 (the LLC sublayer) and 802.3 (the MAC sublayer and physical layer). It operates at both the data link and physical layers, and supports 10 Mb/s through 100 Gb/s."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"What is the minimum and maximum size of a valid Ethernet frame, measured from destination MAC to FCS?",o:["64 to 1518 bytes","46 to 1500 bytes","64 to 9216 bytes","20 to 1460 bytes"],a:0,e:"64 to 1518 bytes. Frames below 64 bytes are collision fragments or runt frames; frames above 1518 are oversized, with baby giants up to 1600 bytes and jumbo frames up to 9216 bytes. Anything outside the valid range is dropped by the receiving device."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"A switch receives a 48-byte frame. What happens to it?",o:["It is dropped, because it is smaller than the 64-byte minimum","It is padded to 64 bytes and forwarded","It is flooded out all ports as a runt broadcast","It is buffered until a second fragment arrives"],a:0,e:"A frame below the 64-byte minimum is a collision fragment or runt frame and the receiving device drops it. This is also why fragment-free cut-through switching stores exactly the first 64 bytes before forwarding — enough to discard collision fragments."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"How long is a MAC address, and how is it usually expressed?",o:["48 bits, written as 12 hexadecimal digits","32 bits, written as 4 decimal octets","64 bits, written as 16 hexadecimal digits","128 bits, written as 8 hextets"],a:0,e:"A MAC address is a 48-bit binary value expressed as 12 hexadecimal digits, at 4 bits per hex digit — for example 00:1B:44:11:3A:B7. 32 bits in dotted decimal is IPv4; 128 bits in eight hextets is IPv6; 64 bits is the length of an IPv6 interface ID."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"Which body assigns the 3-byte Organizationally Unique Identifier that begins every MAC address?",o:["IEEE","IANA","ICANN","IETF"],a:0,e:"The IEEE assigns each vendor a 24-bit OUI and requires two rules: all MAC addresses on a device must use that vendor's OUI as the first 3 bytes, and all addresses with the same OUI must have a unique value in the last 3 bytes. IANA assigns port numbers; ICANN coordinates addresses and domain names."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"Why is a MAC address called a burned-in address (BIA)?",o:["It is encoded permanently into the ROM chip and copied into RAM at startup","It is written into NVRAM by the administrator during setup","It is generated by the operating system each time the host boots","It is leased from a DHCP server at power-on"],a:0,e:"The MAC address is permanently encoded into the ROM chip. When the computer starts up, the first thing the NIC does is copy the MAC address from ROM into RAM. You can view it with ipconfig /all on Windows, or ifconfig on macOS and Linux."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"An IPv4 broadcast packet is encapsulated in an Ethernet frame. What is the destination MAC address?",o:["FF-FF-FF-FF-FF-FF","01-00-5E-00-00-01","00-00-00-00-00-00","The MAC address of the default gateway"],a:0,e:"The broadcast MAC is FF-FF-FF-FF-FF-FF, which is 48 ones in binary. The corresponding IPv4 broadcast packet has all ones in the host portion. Protocols such as DHCP and ARP rely on broadcasts. 01-00-5E is the prefix for multicast MAC addresses, not broadcast."},
{t:"Ethernet",q:"Which MAC address prefix corresponds to an IPv4 multicast group address?",o:["01-00-5E","FF-FF-FF","FE-80-00","00-1B-44"],a:0,e:"A multicast IP address requires a corresponding multicast MAC address beginning 01-00-5E in hexadecimal. IPv4 multicast group addresses run from 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255, and IPv6 multicast addresses begin FF00::/8. FE80 is the IPv6 link-local prefix, not a MAC prefix."},
{t:"Switching",q:"A switch builds its MAC address table by examining which field of incoming frames?",o:["The source MAC address","The destination MAC address","The source IP address","The EtherType field"],a:0,e:"Switches learn from the source MAC address of every incoming frame, recording it with the port number it arrived on. They forward using the destination MAC address. If the source already exists in the table, the switch simply updates the refresh timer for that entry."},
{t:"Switching",q:"For how long does a typical Ethernet switch keep an entry in its MAC address table by default?",o:["5 minutes","30 seconds","200 seconds","24 hours"],a:0,e:"Most Ethernet switches keep an entry for 5 minutes, refreshing the timer each time that source MAC is seen again. 30 seconds is the RIP update interval, 200 seconds is the ICMPv6 Router Advertisement interval, and 24 hours is a typical minimum DHCP lease."},
{t:"Switching",q:"A switch receives a unicast frame whose destination MAC is not in its MAC address table. What does it do?",o:["Floods the frame out all ports except the incoming port","Drops the frame and sends an error to the source","Forwards it only to the default gateway port","Buffers it until the destination sends a frame"],a:0,e:"This is an unknown unicast, and the switch floods it out all ports except the one it arrived on — exactly what it does for broadcast and multicast frames. Once the destination replies, the switch learns its MAC from that reply and can forward directly next time."},
{t:"Switching",q:"Which frame forwarding method receives the entire frame and computes the CRC before forwarding?",o:["Store-and-forward","Fast-forward cut-through","Fragment-free cut-through","Port-based buffering"],a:0,e:"Store-and-forward receives the whole frame, computes the CRC, and forwards only if the CRC is valid — full error checking at the cost of latency. Cut-through methods buffer only enough to read the destination MAC and perform no error checking on the frame."},
{t:"Switching",q:"Which cut-through variant offers the lowest latency and is the most typical form?",o:["Fast-forward switching","Fragment-free switching","Store-and-forward switching","Shared-memory switching"],a:0,e:"Fast-forward switching forwards the packet immediately after reading the destination address, giving the lowest latency, and is the most typical form of cut-through. Fragment-free stores the first 64 bytes first, making it a compromise between store-and-forward and fast-forward."},
{t:"Switching",q:"In which memory buffering method are all frames deposited into a common buffer shared by every port?",o:["Shared memory","Port-based memory","Frame-based memory","Queue-based memory"],a:0,e:"Shared memory places all frames into one common buffer used by all ports. In port-based memory, frames are stored in queues linked to specific incoming and outgoing ports, and a frame is only transmitted once all frames ahead of it have been sent. Buffering is also used when the destination port is congested."},
{t:"Switching",q:"What does the auto-MDIX feature allow a switch port to do?",o:["Detect the cable type attached and configure the interface accordingly","Automatically assign a VLAN based on the connected device","Negotiate a routing protocol with the attached router","Encrypt traffic on the port without configuration"],a:0,e:"Auto-MDIX detects whether a crossover or straight-through cable is attached and configures the interface to match, removing the old requirement to pick a specific cable for switch-to-switch or switch-to-host links. It has been enabled by default since IOS 12.2(18)SE and the command is mdix auto."},
{t:"Switching",q:"On what basis does a Layer 2 Ethernet switch make its forwarding decisions?",o:["Only on Layer 2 Ethernet MAC addresses","On both MAC and IP addresses","On IP addresses and port numbers","On the routing table and administrative distance"],a:0,e:"A Layer 2 switch decides using only MAC addresses. Using IP addresses to choose a path is routing, a Layer 3 function. Port numbers belong to the transport layer, and administrative distance is how a router compares routes learned from different sources."},
{t:"Switching",q:"The MAC address table is sometimes referred to by which other name?",o:["CAM table","ARP table","Routing table","FIB cache"],a:0,e:"It is also called the content addressable memory, or CAM, table. The ARP table is a different structure holding IP-to-MAC mappings on a host or router; the routing table holds network prefixes and next hops on a Layer 3 device."}
]);
window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([
{t:"Network layer",q:"The network layer performs four basic operations. Which list is correct?",o:["Addressing end devices, encapsulation, routing, de-encapsulation","Segmenting, sequencing, acknowledging, retransmitting","Encoding, signalling, framing, error checking","Authentication, authorisation, accounting, auditing"],a:0,e:"Addressing end devices, encapsulation, routing and de-encapsulation. Segmenting and acknowledging belong to the transport layer; encoding and signalling are physical layer functions; AAA is a security framework, not a network layer operation."},
{t:"Network layer",q:"IP is described as connectionless. What does that mean in practice?",o:["It does not establish a connection with the destination before sending","It cannot carry connection-oriented traffic at all","It drops any packet that requires acknowledgement","It uses a different path for every packet by design"],a:0,e:"IP sends without establishing a connection, so no synchronisations or acknowledgements are needed and the destination receives the packet with no prior notification. Connection-oriented behaviour is supplied by another protocol above it, typically TCP at the transport layer."},
{t:"Network layer",q:"Which statement best describes IP as a best-effort protocol?",o:["It does not guarantee delivery and expects no acknowledgements","It retries delivery three times before discarding a packet","It guarantees delivery but not the order of arrival","It guarantees order but not delivery"],a:0,e:"Best effort means IP will not guarantee delivery, has no mechanism to resend lost data, expects no acknowledgements and does not know whether the other device is even operational. This reduced overhead is deliberate, and reliability is left to TCP where it is needed."},
{t:"Network layer",q:"Which of these is NOT something IP can do, making it an unreliable protocol?",o:["Retransmit a packet after an error","Encapsulate a transport layer segment","Carry traffic over copper, fibre or wireless","Add source and destination addresses"],a:0,e:"IP cannot retransmit after an error, cannot manage or fix undelivered or corrupt packets, and cannot realign out-of-sequence packets — it must rely on other protocols for these. It does encapsulate segments, add addresses, and operate over any media, which is what media independence means."},
{t:"Network layer",q:"Fragmentation at Layer 3 occurs in which circumstance, and with what consequence?",o:["When a router moves a packet onto a link with a smaller MTU; it causes latency","When a packet is smaller than 64 bytes; it causes the packet to be padded","Whenever a packet crosses a router; it improves throughput","When TCP requests it; it guarantees delivery"],a:0,e:"Fragmentation is Layer 3 splitting an IPv4 packet into smaller units, typically when a router goes from Ethernet to a slower WAN with a smaller MTU, and fragmenting causes latency. Note that IPv6 does not fragment packets — routers never fragment IPv6."},
{t:"IPv4 header",q:"Which IPv4 header field is a Layer 3 hop count that causes a router to discard the packet when it reaches zero?",o:["Time to Live (TTL)","Header Checksum","Protocol","Differentiated Services"],a:0,e:"TTL is decremented at each router and the packet is discarded at zero, preventing packets circulating forever. The Header Checksum detects corruption in the IPv4 header; Protocol identifies the next-level protocol such as TCP, UDP or ICMP; Differentiated Services is used for QoS."},
{t:"IPv4 header",q:"Which IPv4 header field identifies whether the encapsulated data is TCP, UDP or ICMP?",o:["Protocol","Version","Flags","Identification"],a:0,e:"The Protocol field identifies the next-level protocol. Version is a 4-bit field set to 0100 for IPv4. Identification, Flags and Fragment Offset are the three fields used for fragmentation — and all three are among the fields IPv6 removed or handled differently."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which three limitations of IPv4 does IPv6 exist to overcome?",o:["Address depletion, lack of end-to-end connectivity, increased network complexity","Slow routing, poor security, limited bandwidth","Broadcast storms, collisions, duplex mismatches","Fragmentation, checksums, TTL expiry"],a:0,e:"Those three. Private addressing and NAT extended IPv4's life but ended direct communication with public addressing, and NAT — intended as a temporary solution — adds latency and troubleshooting difficulty by manipulating header addressing. IPv6 answers all three with a 128-bit address space."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"How large is the IPv6 header, and how does it compare with the IPv4 header?",o:["Fixed at 40 bytes — simplified but not smaller","Fixed at 20 bytes — exactly half the size","Variable from 20 to 60 bytes, like IPv4","Fixed at 128 bytes to match the address length"],a:0,e:"The IPv6 header is fixed at 40 bytes and is described as simplified but not smaller. Several IPv4 fields were removed to improve performance: Flags, Fragment Offset and Header Checksum. The IPv4 header is 20 bytes at its minimum."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which IPv6 header field replaces the IPv4 TTL field?",o:["Hop Limit","Flow Label","Traffic Class","Next Header"],a:0,e:"Hop Limit replaces TTL as the Layer 3 hop count. Traffic Class is the QoS field equivalent to DiffServ; Flow Label is a 20-bit field telling devices to handle identical flow labels the same way; Next Header identifies the next-level protocol, equivalent to IPv4's Protocol field."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"IPv6 extension headers have which characteristic?",o:["They are optional and sit between the IPv6 header and the payload","They are mandatory and precede the IPv6 header","They replace the fixed 40-byte header entirely","They are only used by routers, never by hosts"],a:0,e:"Extension headers provide optional network layer information, are optional, and are placed between the IPv6 header and the payload. They may be used for fragmentation, security and mobility support — note that when fragmentation happens in IPv6 it is done by the source, not by routers."},
{t:"Host routing",q:"A host can send packets to three destinations. Which list is correct?",o:["Itself, local hosts, and remote hosts","Itself, the default gateway, and the DNS server","Local hosts, the router, and the ISP","Unicast, multicast, and broadcast addresses"],a:0,e:"Itself (127.0.0.1 in IPv4 or ::1 in IPv6), local hosts on the same LAN, and remote hosts that are not on the same LAN. Packets are always created at the source, and every host device creates its own routing table."},
{t:"Host routing",q:"How does an IPv4 source host determine whether a destination is local or remote?",o:["Using its own IP address and subnet mask together with the destination IP address","Using only the destination IP address","Using the prefix advertised by the local router","By sending an ARP request and waiting for a reply"],a:0,e:"IPv4 uses its own IP address and subnet mask with the destination IP address. IPv6 is the one that uses the network address and prefix advertised by the local router. ARP happens afterwards, once the host already knows whether it needs the destination's MAC or the gateway's."},
{t:"Host routing",q:"Which is NOT a required feature of a default gateway?",o:["It must run a dynamic routing protocol","It must have an IP address in the same range as the rest of the LAN","It must accept data from the LAN and forward traffic off the LAN","It must be able to route to other networks"],a:0,e:"A default gateway need not run any dynamic routing protocol — a static default route is enough. It must be in the same range as the LAN, accept and forward traffic off the LAN, and be able to route to other networks. Without a gateway, or with a bad one, traffic cannot leave the LAN."},
{t:"Host routing",q:"Which two commands display the routing table on a Windows host?",o:["route print and netstat -r","ipconfig /all and arp -a","show ip route and show protocols","tracert and nslookup"],a:0,e:"route print and netstat -r both display it, in three sections: the Interface List with MAC addressing, the IPv4 Routing Table and the IPv6 Routing Table. show ip route is the Cisco IOS equivalent on a router."},
{t:"Routing table",q:"A directly connected route appears in a router's routing table only when which conditions are met?",o:["The interface is active and has addressing configured","A static route has been entered for it","A routing protocol has advertised it","The next-hop router has acknowledged it"],a:0,e:"Directly connected routes are added automatically, provided the interface is active and has addressing. This is why a forgotten no shutdown makes a route silently absent from the table even though the ip address command was entered correctly."},
{t:"Routing table",q:"In show ip route output, what do the codes C and L represent?",o:["C is a directly connected network; L is the local interface IP address","C is a classful route; L is a learned route","C is a connected static route; L is a loopback","C is a child route; L is a link-state route"],a:0,e:"C marks the directly connected network and L marks the local interface address itself, which appears as a /32 host route. S is a manually configured static route, O is OSPF, D is EIGRP, R is RIP, and S* marks a default route."},
{t:"Routing table",q:"Two routes in the table could match a packet's destination. Which one does the router use?",o:["The one with the longest prefix match","The one with the lowest administrative distance","The one with the lowest metric","The one learned most recently"],a:0,e:"Longest prefix match decides which entry applies — the route with the greatest number of matching far-left bits always wins. Administrative distance only decides which source's route gets installed when two protocols learn the same network, and metric only compares routes from the same protocol."},
{t:"Routing table",q:"Which routing table field expresses the trustworthiness of the route source?",o:["Administrative distance","Metric","Next hop","Route timestamp"],a:0,e:"Administrative distance measures trustworthiness, and lower is more trustworthy — a static route at AD 1 beats RIP at AD 120. The metric is the value assigned to reach the network, where lower is the preferred route, and it only compares routes from the same protocol."},
{t:"Routing",q:"Which characteristic describes static routing?",o:["It must be configured and adjusted manually when the topology changes","It automatically finds a new best path when a link goes down","It shares route information with neighbouring routers every 30 seconds","It maintains a database of the whole topology in RAM"],a:0,e:"Static routes are configured manually and must be adjusted manually after a topology change, which makes them good for small non-redundant networks. They are often used alongside a dynamic protocol specifically to configure a default route."},
{t:"ARP",q:"What are the two basic functions of ARP?",o:["Resolving IPv4 addresses to MAC addresses, and maintaining a table of those mappings","Resolving domain names to IP addresses, and caching the results","Assigning IP addresses, and renewing leases","Translating private addresses to public addresses"],a:0,e:"ARP resolves IPv4 addresses to MAC addresses and maintains an ARP table of the mappings. Resolving domain names is DNS; assigning addresses and renewing leases is DHCP; translating private to public addresses is NAT."},
{t:"ARP",q:"A device needs to send a frame and finds no matching entry in its ARP table. What happens next?",o:["It sends an ARP request as a broadcast on the local network","It drops the packet and reports a destination unreachable error","It forwards the frame to the DNS server for resolution","It waits for the ARP cache timer to expire and retries"],a:0,e:"With no entry, the device broadcasts an ARP request; the owner of that IPv4 address replies with an ARP reply, and the requesting device adds the IPv4 and MAC pair to its ARP table. Because every device on the local network receives and processes ARP requests, excessive ARP broadcasts reduce performance."},
{t:"ARP",q:"How are entries removed from an ARP table?",o:["An ARP cache timer expires, or an administrator removes them manually","They are never removed once learned","Only when the interface is shut down","Only when the device receives a gratuitous ARP"],a:0,e:"Entries are not permanent — they are removed when the ARP cache timer expires after a specified period, and the duration differs by operating system. An administrator can also remove them manually. Use show ip arp on a Cisco router or arp -a on a Windows host to view the table."},
{t:"IPv6 ND",q:"IPv6 does not use ARP. Which protocol performs address resolution instead?",o:["ICMPv6 Neighbor Discovery","DHCPv6","SLAAC","Router Information Protocol"],a:0,e:"IPv6 uses the ICMPv6 Neighbor Discovery protocol, which provides address resolution, router discovery and redirection services. SLAAC is a method of address assignment that relies on ND messages; DHCPv6 assigns addresses statefully or provides other configuration information."},
{t:"IPv6 ND",q:"Which pair of ICMPv6 messages is used for device-to-device address resolution and duplicate address detection?",o:["Neighbor Solicitation and Neighbor Advertisement","Router Solicitation and Router Advertisement","Echo Request and Echo Reply","Redirect and Destination Unreachable"],a:0,e:"NS and NA handle device-to-device messaging such as address resolution and DAD, making them the IPv6 equivalents of the ARP request and reply. RS and RA handle device-to-router messaging for router discovery, and ICMPv6 redirect messages let routers suggest a better next hop."}
]);
window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([
{t:"IPv4 addressing",q:"Which range is the complete 172.16.0.0/12 private address block?",o:["172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255","172.16.0.0 to 172.16.255.255","172.16.0.0 to 172.32.255.255","172.0.0.0 to 172.255.255.255"],a:0,e:"The /12 prefix gives 16 consecutive /16 networks, so the block runs from 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255. Stopping at 172.16.255.255 mistakes it for a /16, and 172.32.x.x is outside the block entirely. The other RFC 1918 blocks are 10.0.0.0/8 and 192.168.0.0/16."},
{t:"IPv4 addressing",q:"Why were private addresses introduced in the mid 1990s?",o:["Because of the depletion of IPv4 addresses","To improve routing performance on the internet backbone","To provide encryption for internal traffic","To support classful routing protocols"],a:0,e:"Private addressing, defined by RFC 1918, was a response to IPv4 depletion. Private addresses are not globally routable and are used only in internal networks, so they must be translated to a public IPv4 address by NAT to reach the internet."},
{t:"IPv4 addressing",q:"A Windows PC reports an address of 169.254.88.4. What does this indicate?",o:["It could not reach a DHCP server and self-configured an APIPA address","It has been assigned a valid public address by the ISP","It is using the loopback address to test its own stack","It has been manually configured with a TEST-NET address"],a:0,e:"169.254.0.0/16 is the link-local range, known as APIPA — Automatic Private IP Addressing — used by a Windows client to self-configure when no DHCP server is available. Seeing one is a diagnosis in itself. 127.0.0.0/8 is loopback, and 192.0.2.0/24 is TEST-NET for teaching and documentation."},
{t:"IPv4 addressing",q:"What is the purpose of the 127.0.0.0/8 address range?",o:["Testing on a host whether the TCP/IP configuration is operational","Assigning addresses when DHCP fails","Reserving addresses for documentation and training","Identifying multicast group members"],a:0,e:"Loopback addresses, usually written 127.0.0.1, let a host test whether IP is properly installed locally; the IPv6 equivalent is ::1. APIPA covers DHCP failure, TEST-NET 192.0.2.0/24 covers documentation, and multicast runs from 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255."},
{t:"IPv4 addressing",q:"Under legacy classful addressing, a Class C network was designed to support how many hosts?",o:["A maximum of 254 hosts","Approximately 65 000 hosts","More than 16 million hosts","Exactly 1024 hosts"],a:0,e:"Class C, from 192.0.0.0/24 to 223.255.255.0/24, supports a maximum of 254 hosts. Class B supports approximately 65 000, and Class A more than 16 million. Classful addressing wasted addresses badly, which is what drove the move to classless addressing in the 1990s."},
{t:"IPv4 addressing",q:"What did CIDR allow service providers to do that classful addressing did not?",o:["Allocate addresses on any bit boundary rather than only at class boundaries","Assign more than one address to a single interface","Route private addresses across the public internet","Use the same address in two different networks simultaneously"],a:0,e:"Classless Inter-Domain Routing, pronounced cider, allowed allocation on any address bit boundary — any prefix length — instead of only as a Class A, B or C. This ended the enormous waste of handing an organisation a whole Class B when it needed 300 addresses."},
{t:"IPv4 addressing",q:"Which regional internet registry is responsible for Africa?",o:["AfriNIC","RIPE","APNIC","LACNIC"],a:0,e:"AfriNIC serves Africa. ARIN covers North America, RIPE covers Europe, the Middle East and Central Asia, APNIC covers Asia and the Pacific, and LACNIC covers Latin America and some Caribbean islands. All five receive their allocations from IANA."},
{t:"ICMP",q:"Which ICMP message types does the ping command use?",o:["Echo (Type 8) and echo reply (Type 0)","Destination unreachable (Type 3) and time exceeded (Type 11)","Router solicitation and router advertisement","Neighbor solicitation and neighbor advertisement"],a:0,e:"Ping uses ICMP echo Type 8 and echo reply Type 0. Type 3 destination unreachable is what produces a U in IOS ping output. Windows ping sends four consecutive messages and IOS ping sends five."},
{t:"ICMP",q:"In IOS ping output, what does a U indicate?",o:["A router along the path replied with an ICMP Type 3 destination unreachable","The echo reply was received successfully","Time expired waiting for an echo reply","The destination is unreachable because the cable is unplugged"],a:0,e:"U means a router responded with ICMP Type 3 destination unreachable — possibly because it does not know the direction to the destination network, or could not find the host on it. An exclamation mark is a successful echo reply validating Layer 3 connectivity, and a period means the wait timed out."},
{t:"ICMP",q:"How do you enter extended ping mode on a Cisco router?",o:["Type ping in privileged EXEC mode with no destination address","Type ping extended followed by the destination","Add the keyword repeat to the ping command","Configure it first with the ip ping extended command"],a:0,e:"Extended ping is entered in privileged EXEC mode by typing ping with no destination IP address; IOS then presents prompts to customise the operation, and pressing Enter accepts the indicated defaults. Extended traceroute works the same way, and ping ipv6 is used for IPv6 extended pings."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"Why is the usable host formula 2 to the power n minus 2, while the subnet count formula has no subtraction?",o:["Each subnet loses its network address and broadcast address, but no such loss applies when counting subnets","Two addresses are always reserved for the router and the switch","The subtraction accounts for the first and last subnets being unusable","Two host bits are always borrowed for the mask"],a:0,e:"Every subnet gives up the all-zeros host address as the network address and the all-ones host address as the broadcast address, so two addresses per subnet cannot go to hosts. Counting subnets involves no such reservation, so the number created is simply 2 to the power of the bits borrowed."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"You must subnet 192.168.10.0/24 so each subnet supports at least 50 hosts. How many host bits must you keep?",o:["6","5","7","4"],a:0,e:"Apply 2 to the power n minus 2 greater than or equal to 50. With n = 5 you get 30, which is too few. With n = 6 you get 62, which works. Keeping 6 host bits converts the other 2 into network bits, giving /26, mask 255.255.255.192, and an increment of 64 in the fourth octet."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"A /26 subnet mask written in dotted decimal is which value?",o:["255.255.255.192","255.255.255.224","255.255.255.240","255.255.255.128"],a:0,e:"/26 is 255.255.255.192, with an increment of 64 and 62 usable hosts. 255.255.255.224 is /27 with increment 32; 255.255.255.240 is /28 with increment 16; 255.255.255.128 is /25 with increment 128. A useful shortcut: the interesting octet equals 256 minus the increment."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"For the subnet 192.168.10.64/26, what is the broadcast address?",o:["192.168.10.127","192.168.10.126","192.168.10.128","192.168.10.63"],a:0,e:"With an increment of 64, the next subnet starts at 192.168.10.128, so the broadcast is one less: 192.168.10.127. The usable host range is therefore .65 to .126. The broadcast address is always one less than the next network address."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"You subnet 172.16.0.0/16 to support 1000 hosts per subnet. What is the resulting prefix and increment?",o:["/22, incrementing by 4 in the third octet","/21, incrementing by 8 in the third octet","/22, incrementing by 4 in the fourth octet","/20, incrementing by 16 in the third octet"],a:0,e:"2 to the power 10 minus 2 equals 1022, which covers 1000, so 10 host bits are kept and the mask becomes /22 or 255.255.252.0. The network bits end in the third octet, which retains 2 host bits, so the increment is 2 squared, or 4, in the third octet: 172.16.0.0, 172.16.4.0, 172.16.8.0 and so on."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"A topology needs 5 subnets from 192.168.100.0/24, each with at least 25 addresses. How many bits must be borrowed?",o:["3","2","4","5"],a:0,e:"Borrowing 3 bits creates 2 cubed, or 8 subnets, which covers the 5 required, and leaves 5 host bits giving 2 to the fifth minus 2, or 30 usable hosts — enough for 25. That is a /27 with mask 255.255.255.224 and an increment of 32. Borrowing 4 bits would leave only 14 usable hosts, which is too few."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"Your subnetting calculation produces fewer usable hosts than the requirement. What is the most likely mistake?",o:["You borrowed too many bits","You borrowed too few bits","You forgot to subtract the broadcast address","You used the wrong class of address"],a:0,e:"Too few usable hosts means too many bits were converted from host bits to network bits — go back one power of 2. The Packet Tracer lab flags this explicitly: if your answer is less than the hosts required, you borrowed too many bits."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"In the subnet 10.5.16.0/20, what is the last usable host address?",o:["10.5.31.254","10.5.31.255","10.5.16.254","10.5.32.0"],a:0,e:"A /20 has 4 host bits in the third octet, so the increment is 16 in that octet: this subnet runs from 10.5.16.0 to 10.5.31.255. The broadcast is 10.5.31.255, so the last usable host address is one less, 10.5.31.254."},
{t:"VLSM",q:"In a VLSM design, in what order should subnets be allocated from the address space?",o:["Largest host requirement first, smallest last","Smallest host requirement first, largest last","In the order the LANs appear in the topology diagram","Point-to-point links first, then LANs by size"],a:0,e:"Always allocate largest first. Placing a small subnet at the start of the block fragments the space and leaves no contiguous run big enough for the large LAN later. Point-to-point WAN links, needing just 2 usable addresses, are allocated last as /30s."},
{t:"VLSM",q:"What prefix should be used for a point-to-point link between two routers?",o:["/30","/31","/29","/32"],a:0,e:"A /30 provides 4 addresses total: one network, one broadcast and exactly 2 usable — precisely what two router interfaces need. A /31 gives 2 to the power 1 minus 2, which is zero usable hosts. A /29 gives 6 usable and wastes 4; a /32 is a host route, used for loopbacks."},
{t:"VLSM",q:"From 10.11.48.0/24 you allocate a /26 to the largest LAN. At which address does the next subnet begin?",o:["10.11.48.64","10.11.48.63","10.11.48.32","10.11.48.128"],a:0,e:"A /26 has an increment of 64, so the first subnet is 10.11.48.0 to 10.11.48.63 with .63 as its broadcast address. The next subnet therefore begins at 10.11.48.64. This matches the VLSM lab, where the 60-host LAN takes 10.11.48.0/26 and the 30-host LAN takes 10.11.48.64/27."},
{t:"VLSM",q:"A LAN requires 30 host addresses. Which is the smallest mask that satisfies it exactly?",o:["/27","/28","/26","/29"],a:0,e:"/27 leaves 5 host bits, giving 2 to the fifth minus 2, which is exactly 30 usable addresses. /28 gives only 14, which is too few. /26 gives 62, which works but wastes 32 addresses — in VLSM the point is to use the smallest mask that fits."},
{t:"VLSM",q:"In the course labs, which address in each subnet is assigned to the switch?",o:["The second usable address","The first usable address","The last usable address","The network address"],a:0,e:"The convention across the labs and the case study is: first usable to the router interfaces, last usable on the WAN link to the second router, second usable to the switch SVI, and last usable to the PCs. Knowing this convention saves decision time under exam pressure."},
{t:"VLSM",q:"PC3 sits on 172.16.30.0/28 and its default gateway is the last usable address. What is that address?",o:["172.16.30.14","172.16.30.15","172.16.30.16","172.16.30.30"],a:0,e:"A /28 has an increment of 16, so this subnet runs from 172.16.30.0 to 172.16.30.15. The broadcast is .15, making the last usable address .14. This is exactly the value the ICNF102 case study specifies for the B2 Gi0/0 interface."},
{t:"Subnetting",q:"Given a subnet mask with an increment of 8 in the fourth octet, what is the dotted decimal mask?",o:["255.255.255.248","255.255.255.240","255.255.255.224","255.255.255.252"],a:0,e:"The interesting octet is 256 minus the increment, so 256 minus 8 gives 248 — a /29 with 6 usable hosts. Increment 16 gives 240 (/28), increment 32 gives 224 (/27), and increment 4 gives 252 (/30). This subtraction works in any octet."}
]);
window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which rule correctly describes omitting zeros in an IPv6 address?",o:["Leading zeros in a hextet may be omitted, but trailing zeros may not","Trailing zeros in a hextet may be omitted, but leading zeros may not","Both leading and trailing zeros may be omitted","Zeros may only be omitted using the double colon"],a:0,e:"Only leading zeros may be omitted, because omitting trailing zeros would make the address ambiguous — 0200 shortened to 02 could not be distinguished from 0020. So 2001:0DB8:0001:5270:0127:00AB:CAFE:0E1F becomes 2001:DB8:1:5270:127:AB:CAFE:E1F."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"How many times may the double colon appear in a single IPv6 address?",o:["Once","Twice","As many times as there are zero runs","Never, it is only a display convention"],a:0,e:"The double colon may be used only once, because two of them would produce more than one possible resulting address — the reader could not tell how many zero hextets each represented. It replaces a single contiguous string of one or more all-zero hextets."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Compress 2001:0db8:0000:1111:0000:0000:0000:0200 correctly.",o:["2001:db8:0:1111::200","2001:db8::1111::200","2001:db8:0:1111:0:0:0:200","2001:db8:0000:1111::0200"],a:0,e:"Apply rule 1 everywhere, then spend the single double colon on the longest contiguous zero run — here the three zero hextets near the end. The earlier single zero hextet becomes just 0. Option B uses two double colons, which is illegal, and the others fail to compress fully."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"What is the typical IPv6 prefix length for most LANs, and what is the full possible range?",o:["/64 typical; range 0 to 128","/48 typical; range 0 to 64","/64 typical; range 0 to 64","/128 typical; range 1 to 128"],a:0,e:"The prefix length indicates the network portion and can range from 0 to 128; /64 is typical for most LANs, leaving a 64-bit interface ID. /48 is the typical global routing prefix assigned by a provider, with the subnet ID sitting between the two."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which IPv6 address type is any unicast address that can be assigned to multiple devices?",o:["Anycast","Multicast","Global unicast","Link-local"],a:0,e:"Anycast is a unicast address assigned to multiple devices, so a packet reaches whichever one is nearest. Multicast sends a single packet to multiple destinations and begins FF00::/8. There is no broadcast in IPv6 at all."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which prefix identifies an IPv6 link-local address?",o:["FE80::/10","2000::/3","FC00::/7","FF00::/8"],a:0,e:"Link-local addresses are in FE80::/10 and are confined to a single link. They are created even if the device has never been assigned a global unicast address. 2000::/3 is global unicast, FC00::/7 is unique local, and FF00::/8 is multicast."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which address does a device on an IPv6 link typically use as its default gateway?",o:["The link-local address of the router","The global unicast address of the router","The all-routers multicast address ff02::2","The router's MAC address"],a:0,e:"It is typically the router's link-local address that serves as the default gateway. This holds even when stateful DHCPv6 supplies everything else, because DHCPv6 never provides a default gateway — that information only comes from the Router Advertisement."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which three parts make up an IPv6 global unicast address?",o:["Global routing prefix, subnet ID, interface ID","Network portion, host portion, checksum","Prefix, flow label, hop limit","OUI, FFFE, device identifier"],a:0,e:"Global routing prefix (the network portion assigned by the provider, typically /48), subnet ID (used to subnet within the organisation) and interface ID (equivalent to the host portion of an IPv4 address). OUI, FFFE and device identifier are the three parts of an EUI-64 interface ID specifically."},
{t:"IPv6",q:"Which migration technique encapsulates an IPv6 packet inside an IPv4 packet?",o:["Tunnelling","Dual stack","Translation with NAT64","SLAAC"],a:0,e:"Tunnelling encapsulates IPv6 inside IPv4. Dual stack runs both protocol stacks simultaneously on a device; translation via NAT64 lets IPv6-enabled devices communicate with IPv4 devices. SLAAC is not a migration technique at all — it is a method of dynamic address assignment."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"How often does an IPv6-enabled router send unsolicited ICMPv6 Router Advertisements?",o:["Every 200 seconds","Every 30 seconds","Every 5 minutes","Only when a host sends a Router Solicitation"],a:0,e:"RAs are sent every 200 seconds, and also on demand when a router receives a Router Solicitation. 30 seconds is the RIP update interval and 5 minutes is the default switch MAC table age."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"Which multicast address does a host send a Router Solicitation to, and which does the router send the RA to?",o:["RS to ff02::2, RA to ff02::1","RS to ff02::1, RA to ff02::2","Both to ff02::1","Both to ff02::2"],a:0,e:"A host sends an RS to the all-routers multicast address ff02::2, and the router generates an RA sent to the all-nodes multicast address ff02::1. The host then uses the information in that RA to create a unique IPv6 global unicast address."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"Which combination of RA flags represents the SLAAC-only method, and is it the default?",o:["A=1, O=0, M=0 — yes, this is the default","A=0, O=1, M=1 — yes, this is the default","A=1, O=1, M=0 — no, option 2 is the default","A=0, O=0, M=1 — no, option 3 is the default"],a:0,e:"A=1 tells the client to use the GUA prefix in the RA and create its own interface ID, while O=0 and M=0 mean it should also take DNS, MTU and default gateway information from the RA itself. This is option 1, the default. Option 2 adds a stateless DHCPv6 server and option 3 uses stateful DHCPv6."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"What does the M flag in an RA message signify?",o:["Use a stateful DHCPv6 server to obtain a global unicast address","Use SLAAC to create a global unicast address","Additional information is available from a stateless DHCPv6 server","The MTU value in the message should be ignored"],a:0,e:"M is the Managed Address Configuration flag, directing the host to a stateful DHCPv6 server. A is the Address Autoconfiguration flag for SLAAC, and O is the Other Configuration flag pointing to a stateless DHCPv6 server for information other than the address."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"Why is SLAAC described as stateless?",o:["No server maintains information about which addresses are in use and which are available","It does not require the host to remember its own address","It does not survive a reboot of the host","It cannot supply DNS server information"],a:0,e:"Stateless means there is no server tracking address state. SLAAC lets hosts create their own unique global unicast address without any DHCPv6 server, which matters because not every network has one but every device needs a GUA."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"In the EUI-64 process, which 16-bit hexadecimal value is inserted into the middle of the MAC address?",o:["FFFE","FF02","FE80","FFFF"],a:0,e:"FFFE is inserted between the 24-bit OUI and the 24-bit device identifier taken from the MAC address. Additionally, the 7th bit of the OUI — the Universally/Locally or U/L bit — is reversed. FE80 is the link-local prefix and FFFF relates to broadcast MAC addressing."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"Which interface ID generation method do Windows 10 hosts use by default, and why?",o:["A randomly generated 64-bit value, for privacy reasons","EUI-64, because it guarantees uniqueness","A value supplied by the DHCPv6 server","The last 64 bits of the IPv4 address"],a:0,e:"Windows 10 generates the 64-bit interface ID randomly. Some operating systems default away from EUI-64 due to privacy concerns, because EUI-64 embeds the host's Ethernet MAC address in the IPv6 address, making the device traceable across networks."},
{t:"SLAAC",q:"During Duplicate Address Detection, what does receiving a Neighbor Advertisement mean?",o:["The address is not unique and the host must generate a new interface ID","The address is confirmed unique and may be used","The router has accepted the address","DAD has timed out and must be repeated"],a:0,e:"An NA reply means another device already holds that address, so the host must generate a new interface ID. Silence — no NA received — means the address is virtually guaranteed unique. The NS used for DAD carries a solicited-node multicast address containing the last 24 bits of the host's IPv6 address."},
{t:"Transport",q:"What is the transport layer chiefly responsible for?",o:["Logical communications between applications running on different hosts","Determining the best path between networks","Delivering frames between NICs on the same LAN","Converting data into signals for the media"],a:0,e:"The transport layer handles logical communication between applications and is the link between the application layer and the lower layers responsible for network transmission. It tracks individual conversations, segments and reassembles data, and uses segmentation and multiplexing to interleave conversations."},
{t:"Transport",q:"Which statement correctly contrasts TCP and UDP?",o:["TCP is stateful and connection-oriented; UDP is stateless and connectionless","TCP is connectionless; UDP is connection-oriented","Both establish a session, but only TCP acknowledges data","Both are stateless, but TCP adds sequence numbers"],a:0,e:"TCP is stateful, tracking what it has sent and what has been acknowledged, and establishes a session before forwarding traffic. UDP is stateless and connectionless with no session establishment, providing best-effort delivery with very little overhead and data checking."},
{t:"Transport",q:"How large is the UDP header and how many fields does it contain?",o:["8 bytes, four fields","20 bytes, ten fields","16 bytes, four fields","8 bytes, two fields"],a:0,e:"The UDP header is 8 bytes, or 64 bits, with just four fields: source port, destination port, length and checksum. The TCP header is far more complex, starting at 20 bytes and including sequence numbers, acknowledgement numbers, control bits and window size."},
{t:"Transport",q:"Which TCP header field indicates the number of bytes that can be accepted at one time?",o:["Window size","Sequence number","Acknowledgment number","Header length"],a:0,e:"Window size is a 16-bit field expressing how much the receiver can accept, and it is the mechanism behind flow control and sliding windows. The 32-bit sequence number is used for data reassembly, and the 32-bit acknowledgment number indicates the next byte expected from the source."},
{t:"Transport",q:"Which applications typically use UDP because they tolerate loss but not delay?",o:["VoIP and live streaming video","HTTP and HTTPS web browsing","FTP file transfers","SMTP email delivery"],a:0,e:"Live video and multimedia applications tolerate some data loss but require little or no delay, making UDP the right choice. UDP is also used for simple request-and-reply applications such as DNS and DHCP, and where the application handles reliability itself, such as SNMP and TFTP."},
{t:"Ports",q:"What exactly is a socket?",o:["The combination of an IP address and a port number","A physical connector on a network interface card","A pair of MAC addresses in a frame header","A reserved range of port numbers"],a:0,e:"A socket is the combination of an IP address and a port number — source IP with source port, or destination IP with destination port. Sockets let multiple processes on one client distinguish themselves, and let multiple connections to one server process be told apart."},
{t:"Ports",q:"Which port range is described as private or dynamic, also known as ephemeral?",o:["49152 to 65535","0 to 1023","1024 to 49151","1 to 1024"],a:0,e:"49152 to 65535 are private or dynamic, assigned by the client operating system when a connection is initiated and used to identify the client application. 0 to 1023 are well-known ports for common services; 1024 to 49151 are registered ports assigned by IANA on request."},
{t:"Ports",q:"Cisco registered port 1812 for its RADIUS server authentication process. Which port group is that?",o:["Registered ports","Well-known ports","Private and dynamic ports","Reserved system ports"],a:0,e:"Registered ports run from 1024 to 49151 and are assigned by IANA to a requesting entity for specific processes — typically applications a user chose to install rather than universal services. Well-known ports below 1024 cover the common services such as HTTP, DNS and SMTP."}
]);
window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([
{t:"Ports",q:"Which ports does FTP use, and what does each carry?",o:["21 for control traffic and 20 for the data transfer","20 for control traffic and 21 for the data transfer","21 for both, using two separate sessions","69 for control and 20 for data"],a:0,e:"The client first establishes a control connection on TCP port 21 for commands and server replies, then a second connection on TCP port 20 for the actual data transfer, created each time there is data to move. Port 69 is TFTP, which uses UDP and a single connection."},
{t:"Ports",q:"Which protocol and port combination is used to SEND email?",o:["SMTP on TCP 25","POP3 on TCP 110","IMAP on TCP 143","SMTP on UDP 25"],a:0,e:"SMTP sends mail over TCP port 25. POP3 on port 110 and IMAP on port 143 are both used by clients to retrieve mail. The key distinction between the two retrieval protocols is that POP deletes messages from the server while IMAP keeps them and synchronises changes."},
{t:"TCP",q:"What are the three steps of the TCP three-way handshake, in order?",o:["Client requests a session; server acknowledges and requests its own; client acknowledges","Client sends SYN; server sends FIN; client sends ACK","Client sends data; server acknowledges; client closes","Server offers a session; client accepts; server confirms"],a:0,e:"The client requests a client-to-server session (SYN), the server acknowledges it and requests a server-to-client session (SYN, ACK), and the client acknowledges the server-to-client session (ACK). Note that establishment takes three steps while termination takes four."},
{t:"TCP",q:"How many steps does TCP session termination take, and why?",o:["Four, because each direction must be closed independently with a FIN and an ACK","Three, mirroring the handshake","Two, a FIN and an ACK","One, a single RST from either side"],a:0,e:"Four steps: the client sends FIN, the server ACKs it, the server sends its own FIN, and the client ACKs that. Each direction of the session is closed independently, which is why there is no three-step shortcut. RST is used to reset a connection after an error or timeout, not for normal closure."},
{t:"TCP",q:"Which TCP control flag synchronises sequence numbers during connection establishment?",o:["SYN","ACK","PSH","URG"],a:0,e:"SYN synchronises sequence numbers and is used in connection establishment. ACK is the acknowledgment flag used in both establishment and termination, FIN signals no more data from the sender during termination, RST resets after an error or timeout, PSH is the push function and URG marks the urgent pointer as significant."},
{t:"TCP",q:"Which of the following is NOT one of the three functions of the three-way handshake?",o:["It encrypts the session keys for the conversation","It establishes that the destination device is present on the network","It verifies the destination has an active service on that port","It informs the destination that the client intends to establish a session on that port"],a:0,e:"The handshake performs no encryption — TCP has no security function, which is why HTTPS layers TLS above it. Its three functions are confirming the destination exists, confirming an active service is accepting requests on that port, and announcing the intent to open a session."},
{t:"TCP",q:"What is selective acknowledgment (SACK) and when is it negotiated?",o:["It lets the receiver acknowledge discontinuous segments; negotiated during the three-way handshake","It acknowledges every segment individually; negotiated per segment","It disables acknowledgements entirely; negotiated at session termination","It acknowledges only the final segment; negotiated by the application"],a:0,e:"SACK is an optional feature negotiated during the three-way handshake. If both hosts support it, the receiver can explicitly acknowledge which segments were received including discontinuous ones, so only the genuinely missing bytes are retransmitted rather than everything after the gap."},
{t:"TCP",q:"A common IPv4 MSS is 1460 bytes. How is that figure derived?",o:["1500-byte Ethernet MTU minus a 20-byte IPv4 header minus a 20-byte TCP header","1500-byte MTU minus a 40-byte IPv6 header","1518-byte maximum frame minus a 58-byte overhead","1460 is the fixed value defined in the TCP standard"],a:0,e:"A host subtracts the IP and TCP headers from the Ethernet MTU: 1500 minus 20 for IPv4 and 20 for TCP leaves 1460 bytes of data. MSS is the maximum amount of data the destination device can receive, not a fixed constant — change the MTU or use IPv6 and the figure changes."},
{t:"TCP",q:"What is the process called where the destination acknowledges bytes as it processes them and the source continually adjusts its send window?",o:["Sliding windows","Congestion avoidance","Selective acknowledgment","Multiplexing"],a:0,e:"Sliding windows is the continual adjustment of the send window in response to acknowledgements. Congestion avoidance is a separate set of mechanisms, timers and algorithms responding to routers discarding packets when overloaded."},
{t:"UDP",q:"How does UDP handle datagrams that arrive out of order?",o:["It reassembles them in the order received and forwards them to the application","It buffers them and reorders using sequence numbers","It discards any datagram arriving out of order","It requests retransmission from the source"],a:0,e:"UDP does not track sequence numbers and has no way to reorder datagrams, so it simply reassembles data in the order received and forwards it to the application. Any reordering or reliability must be handled by the application itself."},
{t:"UDP",q:"How does a UDP client choose its source port, and what happens to it during the transaction?",o:["It selects dynamically from the private range and reuses the same pair throughout","It uses the same well-known port as the server","It selects a new port for every datagram sent","It requests a port from the server during a handshake"],a:0,e:"The client dynamically selects a source port from the private or dynamic range, with the destination usually being the server's well-known or registered port. Once chosen, the same pair of ports appears in the header of every datagram in that transaction. UDP has no handshake at all."},
{t:"Application",q:"Which three functions belong to the presentation layer?",o:["Formatting, compressing and encrypting data","Segmenting, sequencing and acknowledging","Addressing, routing and forwarding","Framing, error detection and media access"],a:0,e:"The presentation layer formats data into a compatible form for the destination, compresses data so the destination can decompress it, and encrypts for transmission with decryption on receipt. The session layer creates and maintains dialogs and restarts sessions that are disrupted or long idle."},
{t:"Application",q:"In a peer-to-peer network, when does a device act as a client rather than a server?",o:["Roles are set on a per-request basis and a peer can be both simultaneously","A peer is permanently assigned one role at setup","It acts as a client only when the index server is unavailable","It is always a client unless a dedicated server fails"],a:0,e:"Every connected peer can function as both server and client, and roles are set per request — one machine may serve one transaction while simultaneously acting as a client for another. Some P2P applications use a hybrid system where peers consult an index server to locate a resource stored on another peer."},
{t:"Application",q:"Which HTTP message type uploads a resource such as an image to the web server?",o:["PUT","GET","POST","HEAD"],a:0,e:"PUT uploads resources or content such as an image. GET is a client request for data, used when the browser asks for an HTML page. POST uploads data files such as form data. HTTP is not a secure protocol, so HTTPS should be used for secure communication across the internet."},
{t:"Application",q:"Which describes the difference between POP and IMAP correctly?",o:["POP deletes messages from the server after download; IMAP keeps them and synchronises deletions","POP keeps messages on the server; IMAP deletes them","Both delete messages, but IMAP is encrypted","Both keep messages, but POP is faster"],a:0,e:"With POP the messages are deleted on the server once downloaded, which is why POP is not recommended where a centralised backup is needed. With IMAP copies are downloaded, originals stay on the server until manually deleted, and when the user deletes a message the server synchronises that action."},
{t:"Application",q:"SMTP encounters a destination mail server that is offline. What does it do?",o:["It spools the message to be sent at a later time","It returns the message to the sender immediately","It converts the message to POP format and stores it locally","It discards the message and logs an error"],a:0,e:"SMTP spools messages to be sent later if the destination server is offline or busy — part of email being a store-and-forward method. When the server does receive a message it either places it in a local account if the recipient is local, or forwards it to another mail server for delivery."},
{t:"DNS",q:"Which DNS resource record type resolves to an end device IPv6 address?",o:["AAAA","A","NS","MX"],a:0,e:"AAAA, pronounced quad-A, resolves an IPv6 address. A resolves an IPv4 address, NS identifies an authoritative name server, and MX is a mail exchange record. These records contain the name, address and type of record."},
{t:"DNS",q:"What happens when a DNS server receives a request for a name outside its own DNS zone?",o:["It forwards the request to another DNS server within the proper zone","It returns a negative response to the client immediately","It broadcasts the query to all servers on the local network","It creates a new record for the name and returns its own address"],a:0,e:"DNS is hierarchical: each server maintains a specific database file and is only responsible for name-to-IP mappings for its small portion of the whole structure, forwarding anything outside its zone. After a match is returned, the server temporarily caches the address in case the same name is requested again."},
{t:"DNS",q:"Which four sections make up a DNS message?",o:["Question, answer, authority, additional","Header, body, footer, checksum","Query, response, error, transfer","Name, address, type, TTL"],a:0,e:"DNS uses the same message format between servers for all client queries, server responses, error messages and resource record transfers: Question (the question for the name server), Answer (records answering it), Authority (records pointing toward an authority) and Additional (records holding extra information)."},
{t:"DNS",q:"What does the nslookup command do when issued with no arguments?",o:["Displays the default DNS server configured for the host and waits for a name","Resolves the local host name to its IP address","Clears the DNS resolver cache","Lists every record held by the local DNS server"],a:0,e:"It displays the default DNS server configured on the device, after which a host or domain name can be entered at the prompt. It is used to manually query DNS and to troubleshoot name resolution issues or verify name server status. Clearing the cache is a different task; ipconfig /displaydns shows cached entries."},
{t:"DHCP",q:"What are the four DHCP lease acquisition messages, in order?",o:["DHCPDISCOVER, DHCPOFFER, DHCPREQUEST, DHCPACK","DHCPREQUEST, DHCPOFFER, DHCPDISCOVER, DHCPACK","DHCPDISCOVER, DHCPREQUEST, DHCPOFFER, DHCPACK","DHCPOFFER, DHCPDISCOVER, DHCPACK, DHCPREQUEST"],a:0,e:"Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. The client broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER to identify available servers; a server replies with DHCPOFFER; the client sends DHCPREQUEST naming the explicit server and offer it accepts; the server returns DHCPACK finalising the lease."},
{t:"DHCP",q:"A client receives DHCPOFFER messages from two different servers. What does it do?",o:["It must choose one of them","It accepts both and uses two addresses","It discards both and restarts the process","It asks the router to arbitrate"],a:0,e:"If a client receives more than one offer because multiple DHCP servers exist on the network, it must choose one, and its DHCPREQUEST identifies the explicit server and lease offer being accepted. If that offer is no longer valid the server replies with a DHCPNAK and the process restarts with a new DHCPDISCOVER."},
{t:"DHCP",q:"Which two messages are used to renew a lease, and how is the first sent?",o:["DHCPREQUEST then DHCPACK; the request is sent unicast directly to the original server","DHCPDISCOVER then DHCPACK; the discover is broadcast","DHCPREQUEST then DHCPOFFER; the request is broadcast","DHCPRENEW then DHCPACK; the renew is unicast"],a:0,e:"Before the lease expires the client sends a DHCPREQUEST directly to the server that originally offered the address, and the server verifies the lease by returning a DHCPACK. If no DHCPACK arrives within a specified time, the client broadcasts another DHCPREQUEST so another server can extend the lease."},
{t:"DHCP",q:"What are the DHCPv6 message equivalents?",o:["SOLICIT, ADVERTISE, INFORMATION REQUEST, REPLY","DISCOVER6, OFFER6, REQUEST6, ACK6","RS, RA, NS, NA","BIND, OFFER, CONFIRM, RELEASE"],a:0,e:"DHCPv6 uses SOLICIT, ADVERTISE, INFORMATION REQUEST and REPLY. RS, RA, NS and NA are ICMPv6 Neighbor Discovery messages, a separate mechanism — although the RA is what supplies the default gateway, since DHCPv6 does not."},
{t:"Application",q:"What distinguishes SMB from FTP in how clients connect?",o:["SMB clients establish a long-term connection and use resources as though local","SMB uses two ports while FTP uses one","SMB is connectionless while FTP is connection-oriented","SMB can only transfer files, while FTP can also print"],a:0,e:"With SMB, clients establish a long-term connection and can access server resources as though they were local to the client host. FTP creates a fresh data connection each time data is transferred. The three functions of SMB messages are starting, authenticating and terminating sessions; controlling file and printer access; and letting applications send or receive messages."}
]);
window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([
{t:"DHCP config",q:"Which command excludes a range of addresses from a Cisco IOS DHCPv4 pool?",o:["ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.10.1 192.168.10.10","ip dhcp pool exclude 192.168.10.1 192.168.10.10","no ip dhcp 192.168.10.1 192.168.10.10","ip dhcp reserved 192.168.10.1 192.168.10.10"],a:0,e:"ip dhcp excluded-address takes a low address and an optional high address, and may be entered multiple times. Exclusions must cover anything statically assigned — routers, servers, printers and management interfaces — or the server will hand out addresses already in use, causing duplicate-address errors."},
{t:"DHCP config",q:"Which prompt confirms you are in DHCP pool configuration mode?",o:["Router(dhcp-config)#","Router(config-dhcp)#","Router(config-pool)#","Router(config-if)#"],a:0,e:"The ip dhcp pool command creates the pool and moves the router into DHCP configuration mode, identified by Router(dhcp-config)#. Router(config-if)# is interface configuration mode, used for commands such as ip helper-address and ip address dhcp."},
{t:"DHCP config",q:"Within a DHCP pool, which command defines the default gateway handed to clients?",o:["default-router","default-gateway","ip default-gateway","gateway-address"],a:0,e:"default-router, which accepts up to eight addresses. ip default-gateway is a global command used on a switch to give its own management interface a way off the LAN, not a DHCP pool command. Other pool commands include network, dns-server, domain-name, lease and netbios-name-server."},
{t:"DHCP config",q:"Which command displays every IPv4-address-to-MAC-address binding the DHCP service has issued?",o:["show ip dhcp binding","show ip dhcp server statistics","show running-config | section dhcp","show ip dhcp pool"],a:0,e:"show ip dhcp binding lists the address-to-MAC bindings with lease expiry and type. show ip dhcp server statistics gives counts of messages sent and received, and show running-config | section dhcp displays just the DHCP commands configured on the router."},
{t:"DHCP config",q:"PC1 on the R1 LAN cannot obtain an address from a DHCP server located on R2's network. What is the fix?",o:["Configure ip helper-address with the server's IP on R1's LAN interface","Configure a static route on R1 pointing to the DHCP server","Enable ip dhcp pool on R1 for the same network","Set the PC to use a static address instead"],a:0,e:"Clients find servers using broadcasts, and routers do not forward broadcasts. ip helper-address on the interface the clients are attached to makes R1 accept the broadcast requests and forward them as a unicast to the server. Verify the configuration with show ip interface."},
{t:"DHCP config",q:"Besides DHCP, ip helper-address forwards seven other UDP services by default. Which is among them?",o:["TFTP on port 69","HTTP on port 80","SMTP on port 25","SSH on port 22"],a:0,e:"The eight relayed UDP services are Time (37), TACACS (49), DNS (53), DHCP/BOOTP server (67), DHCP/BOOTP client (68), TFTP (69), NetBIOS name service (137) and NetBIOS datagram service (138). HTTP, SMTP and SSH all use TCP and are not broadcast services, so they are never relayed."},
{t:"DHCP config",q:"Which command configures a router's Ethernet interface to obtain its address from an ISP's DHCP server?",o:["ip address dhcp","ip dhcp client enable","ip helper-address dhcp","ip address negotiated"],a:0,e:"ip address dhcp in interface configuration mode makes the router a DHCP client, typical for SOHO and branch routers connecting to a cable or DSL modem. Follow it with no shutdown, then confirm with show ip interface brief that an address was allocated."},
{t:"NAT",q:"Your host is configured with 10.1.1.5 and appears on the internet as 203.0.113.9. Name both addresses correctly.",o:["10.1.1.5 is the inside local; 203.0.113.9 is the inside global","10.1.1.5 is the inside global; 203.0.113.9 is the inside local","10.1.1.5 is the outside local; 203.0.113.9 is the outside global","Both are inside global addresses"],a:0,e:"Inside local is the private address the host actually holds; inside global is the public address it appears as to the outside world. Outside local and outside global describe an external device's address as seen from inside and its real public address respectively."},
{t:"NAT",q:"Which NAT type maps multiple private addresses to a single public address using port numbers?",o:["PAT, also known as NAT overload","Static NAT","Dynamic NAT","NAT64"],a:0,e:"PAT maps many private addresses to one public address (or a few), using port numbers to forward response packets to the correct internal device, and validates that incoming packets were requested. Static NAT is a fixed one-to-one mapping, and dynamic NAT draws one-to-one mappings from a pool."},
{t:"NAT",q:"Under dynamic NAT, what happens when every address in the pool is already in use?",o:["Further inside devices cannot communicate with any external network","The router begins overloading addresses automatically","Traffic is forwarded untranslated with its private address","The oldest translation is dropped to make room"],a:0,e:"With dynamic NAT a single inside address is translated to a single outside address on a first-come, first-served basis, so the pool must be large enough for all simultaneous sessions. When it is exhausted, a device is simply unable to communicate externally — adding overload is what turns this into PAT."},
{t:"NAT",q:"Which command creates a static NAT mapping?",o:["ip nat inside source static 172.16.16.1 64.100.50.1","ip nat static inside 172.16.16.1 64.100.50.1","ip nat pool static 172.16.16.1 64.100.50.1","ip nat inside source list 1 static 64.100.50.1"],a:0,e:"ip nat inside source static local-ip global-ip creates the mapping. It must be paired with ip nat inside on the internal interface and ip nat outside on the external one, otherwise no translation occurs no matter how correct the mapping statement is."},
{t:"NAT",q:"What single keyword converts a dynamic NAT configuration into PAT?",o:["overload","pat","many-to-one","port"],a:0,e:"Adding overload to the end of the ip nat inside source list statement turns dynamic NAT into PAT. The two configurations are otherwise identical — pool, access list, binding statement and interface designations — which is why the keyword is such a common exam target."},
{t:"NAT",q:"Which command verifies the active NAT translation table?",o:["show ip nat translations","show ip nat pool","show run | include nat","show ip nat interfaces"],a:0,e:"show ip nat translations displays the active table, with a verbose variant adding timers and detail, and show ip nat statistics giving hit and miss counts. show run | include nat is useful for quickly confirming whether any NAT commands exist at all. Counters are reset with clear ip nat statistics."},
{t:"NAT",q:"Which is listed as a disadvantage of NAT?",o:["End-to-end IP traceability is lost","It wastes registered public addresses","It prevents internal hosts from reaching the internet","It requires IPv6 on all internal hosts"],a:0,e:"The disadvantages are degraded performance, degraded end-to-end functionality, lost end-to-end IP traceability, more complicated tunnelling, and disrupted TCP connection initiation. Its advantages include conserving registered addresses, flexibility, internal addressing consistency and security."},
{t:"VLANs",q:"How many broadcast domains do four VLANs on a single switch create?",o:["Four","One","Eight","Depends on the number of ports"],a:0,e:"Each VLAN is its own broadcast domain, and a single VLAN can span multiple physical LAN segments. VLANs are mutually isolated, so packets can only pass between them via a Layer 3 device such as a router — which is the entire reason inter-VLAN routing exists."},
{t:"VLANs",q:"What is a VLAN trunk?",o:["A point-to-point link that carries more than one VLAN","A port permanently assigned to the default VLAN","A redundant link between two access ports","A physical bundle of cables between switches"],a:0,e:"A trunk is a point-to-point link carrying more than one VLAN, established between switches to support communication within VLANs that span them. Importantly, trunk ports are not associated with any VLAN themselves."},
{t:"VLANs",q:"Which VLAN carries all untagged traffic, and what is its default ID?",o:["The native VLAN, VLAN 1 by default","The default VLAN, VLAN 0 by default","The management VLAN, VLAN 99 by default","The data VLAN, VLAN 10 by default"],a:0,e:"The native VLAN carries untagged traffic — traffic that does not originate from a VLAN port, such as from switches — and is VLAN 1 by default. The default VLAN is also VLAN 1, of which all switch ports are members initially, but the two terms describe different roles."},
{t:"VLANs",q:"Where are normal-range VLAN configurations stored?",o:["In vlan.dat in flash","In the running configuration in NVRAM","In the startup configuration only","In RAM, lost on reboot"],a:0,e:"Normal-range VLANs, numbered 1 to 1005, are stored in vlan.dat in flash. Extended-range VLANs, 1006 to 4096, are stored in the running configuration, and VTP does not learn extended VLANs. IDs 1002 to 1005 are reserved for legacy Token Ring and FDDI and cannot be removed."},
{t:"VLANs",q:"Which commands assign switch port Fa0/5 to VLAN 20 as an access link?",o:["switchport mode access, then switchport access vlan 20","switchport mode trunk, then switchport trunk vlan 20","vlan 20, then interface Fa0/5","switchport vlan 20 access"],a:0,e:"First set the mode with switchport mode access, then assign the VLAN with switchport access vlan 20. The VLAN itself must already exist, created in global configuration with vlan 20 and optionally named. Trunk commands are for links carrying multiple VLANs between devices."},
{t:"Inter-VLAN",q:"Which inter-VLAN routing option uses sub-interfaces on a single physical router interface?",o:["Router-on-a-stick","Legacy inter-VLAN routing","Layer 3 switching using SVIs","Trunk bridging"],a:0,e:"Router-on-a-stick carries all VLANs over one trunk divided into sub-interfaces, one per VLAN. Legacy inter-VLAN routing uses one physical router interface per VLAN and does not scale. Layer 3 switching uses switched virtual interfaces on the switch itself, requiring no external router."},
{t:"Inter-VLAN",q:"In a router-on-a-stick configuration, what must be applied to each sub-interface before its IP address?",o:["encapsulation dot1Q with the VLAN ID","switchport mode trunk","no shutdown","ip default-gateway"],a:0,e:"Each sub-interface needs encapsulation dot1Q vlan-id before the ip address command, otherwise the router cannot tag frames for that VLAN. The physical interface itself needs no shutdown but gets no IP address, and switchport commands belong on the switch, not the router."},
{t:"Inter-VLAN",q:"In router-on-a-stick, what must the switch port connecting to the router be configured as?",o:["A trunk port","An access port in VLAN 1","A routed port","A passive interface"],a:0,e:"The switch-side port must be a trunk so it can carry all the tagged VLANs to the router. Leaving it as an access port is one of the three most common mistakes, along with forgetting no shutdown on the router's physical interface and omitting encapsulation dot1Q."},
{t:"RIP",q:"What are RIPv2's metric, administrative distance and update interval?",o:["Hop count, 120, every 30 seconds","Bandwidth, 110, every 30 seconds","Hop count, 90, every 60 seconds","Cost, 120, every 90 seconds"],a:0,e:"RIPv2 is a distance vector protocol using hop count as its metric, with administrative distance 120 and updates every 30 seconds. AD 110 belongs to OSPF and 90 to EIGRP. The administrative distance matters because a static route at AD 1 will always beat a RIP route to the same network."},
{t:"RIP",q:"Why must no auto-summary be configured on RIPv2 in a VLSM design?",o:["RIPv2 summarises at classful boundaries by default, which destroys the VLSM subnet information","RIPv2 cannot advertise any network without it","It is required before the version 2 command will take effect","It reduces the update interval from 30 to 15 seconds"],a:0,e:"RIPv2 automatically summarises networks at major network boundaries. With subnets of different lengths inside one classful network, summarising discards exactly the information VLSM depends on. Verify that it is off with show ip protocols."},
{t:"RIP",q:"What does the passive-interface command do in RIP configuration?",o:["Stops routing updates leaving that interface, while still advertising its network to other routers","Stops the interface forwarding any traffic","Removes the network from the RIP process entirely","Converts the interface into a trunk"],a:0,e:"It stops routing updates being sent out the named interface but still allows that network to be advertised to other routers. Updates go out all RIP-enabled interfaces by default, and sending them onto LANs with no routers wastes bandwidth, wastes resources and is a security risk."}
]);
